# Inbox: known limits left on purpose

Each was seen in review or a probe and judged not worth a change yet. Revisit when the
condition in it shows up.

- **Lock wait under load.** 17 processes at once (8 writers, 6 takers, 3 offers) gave
  two "the inbox is busy" errors after the 5 s wait. A write does about six fsyncs under
  `inbox.lock`; the staged file's directory sync is the one to drop if real use hits
  this. The error is safe to repeat.
- **One interpreter per filed message.** The detached `inbox.received` process costs
  about 0.4 s of CPU with no rows, and nothing bounds how many run at once. A burst of
  messages against a row that waits its full timeout stacks them; measure on a
  Raspberry Pi before a router runs there.
- **Every terminal of one machine is the same taker.** A terminal has no ID, only its
  machine, so a second `take` from any terminal of that machine succeeds and prints the
  message. `take` is exclusive between sessions and between machines' terminals, not
  between two people or two daemons on one machine that pass no `--via-…`.
- **`offer` doesn't check that the caller may see the message.** A primary in project A
  can offer project B's message to a B session without `--all`. The notice carries
  `from`, `via` and the project, never the text.
- **A repeat filing looks like a new one.** A `meridian message inbox` that matches an
  existing message by `--via-program`/`--via-ref` prints the same result as the first;
  `--json` has no `repeat` field.
- **A filing without `--via-ref` can be filed twice.** `meridian message <machine>/inbox`
  whose call got no answer fails with `maybe_delivered`; only a filing with
  `--via-program` and `--via-ref` matches the first on the repeat. Its error text says
  "may have been delivered", the session route's words, not "filed".
- **`visible` leaves U+2028 and U+2029 raw and doesn't escape a backslash.** A sender's
  literal `\x1b` prints like an escaped ESC, so text output can't be turned back into
  the text; `--json` is the exact form.
- **An unreadable claim in `list --taken`** lists the message with no "taken" part
  (`"taken": null`), though `take` reports it taken. Only a hand-edited file reaches it.
- **Prune runs in the foreground** of the day's first `meridian inbox` command, after its
  output; a caller that reads to end of file waits for that pass.
- **The notice names this machine by its own name.** An offer to a session on another
  machine prints `meridian inbox take m412 --machine <this machine's name>`. A machine
  that saved this one under another name, or not at all, can't run it as printed; the
  caller block's sender frame is resolved by machine ID, the notice text is not.
- **A sender on another machine has no `agent`.** The caller block of a machine call
  carries no profile name, so `from` for a session there is its machine, chat ID and
  project only.
- **A terminal's repeated `release` can drop a claim made in between.** A terminal may
  release any claim. If its `release` is cut off after it was sent and another session
  takes the message before the repeat, the repeat ("Run it again: a repeat is safe")
  releases that session's claim. A daemon that releases on behalf of people is the caller
  this fits; a release that names the claim it means to drop would close it.
- **An unknown session on another machine is `failed`, not `not_found`.** `offer m412
  <machine>/<id>` asks that machine's `spawn show`, whose error carries no kind; the text
  says "not found". It becomes `not_found` when `spawn show` prints kinds.
- **A session whose ID isn't machine-wide gets no reply address across machines.** Its
  way back is `<id> --machine <name> -C <path>`, and a message's `reply_to` is one
  address. `inbox read` says it has no reply address.
- **The offer notice's command is only right where this build runs.** A session on a
  machine that runs an older Meridian, or a harness whose `meridian` on `PATH` is older,
  is told to run `meridian inbox take … --machine …` and has no such command.
